            "creator": "kevinushey",
            "bug_id": 19362,
            "text": "## Summary\n\n`terms()` writes one byte past a stack buffer when a model term starts with the empty symbol (a missing argument). Fixes a reproducible stack buffer overflow, low impact: only a sanitizer shows it.\n\n## Details\n\nStep 5 of `termsform()` in `src/library/stats/src/model.c` sizes each term label counting a `:` separator only when the length so far is positive, then writes one before every variable after the first. The empty symbol has a zero-length name, so a term starting with it gets a buffer one byte short, and the terminating NUL lands past it. OSS-Fuzz reached it in R-devel r90940 through `update.formula()`; a formula built with `substitute()` from a missing argument has the same term.\n\nReproducible example: attached as `terms-label-buffer-repro.R`; an ASan build reports a dynamic-stack-buffer-overflow in `termsform()`, while a regular build returns the label `:b` with no visible effect.\n\nProposed patch: attached as `terms-label-buffer.diff` (against r90968). It counts each name plus one byte, so the labels do not change, and adds a test to `tests/reg-fuzz.R`. `make check` passes, and the reproducer runs clean under ASan.\n\nThe patch is also submitted as https://github.com/r-devel/r-svn/pull/527, where CI results can be seen.\n\n## AI Disclosure\n\nThis patch was prepared with the assistance of Claude Code. The issue was discovered while fuzzing model formulas with OSS-Fuzz.",
